**Verified** (11 probe cases + the suite): odd/even lengths, single element, all-duplicates, negatives, floats, unsorted input, non-mutation of the caller's array. All correct, and `bash check.sh` + `node --test` pass (2/2, exit 0).

**One real regression found and fixed** — in my earlier fix, `median([])` changed from returning `undefined` (old behavior: `sorted[0]` of an empty array) to `NaN`. That's the only behavior change beyond the even-length bug, and it could break a caller using `median(xs) ?? fallback`. `stats.js` now returns `undefined` for empty input, matching the original code. Only `test/median.test.js` imports this module (confirmed by grep), so nothing else in the folder was affected.

**Checked and dismissed:** the `(a+b)/2` even-average loses no real precision — I probed `median([2**53, 2**53+2])` and it returns the best value a JS number can represent (the exact median isn't representable as a double there).

Current `stats.js`:
```js
function median(values) {
  const sorted = [...values].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  if (sorted.length === 0) return undefined;
  const mid = Math.floor(sorted.length / 2);
  if (sorted.length % 2 === 0) {
    return (sorted[mid - 1] + sorted[mid]) / 2;
  }
  return sorted[mid];
}
```

If your colleague had a specific case in mind beyond the empty array, share it and I'll test it directly — the probe cases above are what I could construct, and none of them break.

